                <pb facs="0166" n="84" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg84"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.3311/1.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chinese Minister to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Imperial Chinese Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-06-19"><hi rendition="#italic">June 19,
                        1911</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I am instructed by my Government to inform
                    you that the Chinese cruiser <hi rendition="#italic">Hai Chi</hi>, which is now
                    in England, has been ordered to make a visit to the United States after taking
                    part in the ceremonies connected with the coronation of King George V. This is
                    the first instance of a Chinese public vessel visiting the American shores. I
                    have the honor to inquire if there are special rules and formalities to be
                    observed on such an occasion.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chang yin tang.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
